<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muzaka family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Muzaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muzaka were an Albanian noble family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myzeqe">Myzeqe plain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>. In the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a> received the Byzantine title of despot, and the family ruled Berat until the Ottoman conquest of 1417. Its members later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>. In 1510 Gjon Muzaka, then in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, wrote a short history of the family, an important source for medieval Albania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muzaka-family.jpg" alt="Muzaka family" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustration by Kj1595 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stema_e_Muzakajve.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family came from the lower Opar region, where many of its ancestral burial grounds lie around the village of Lavdar; ethnographic sources name Lal Muzaka as its earliest known member. Gjon Muzaka claimed that the name came from the Myzeqë region, itself named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> through a corruption of Molossi. The earliest mention of the family is in the work of the Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a>, who around 1090 describes a Muzaka as a loyal commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>. The family's coat of arms was a two-headed eagle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the turn of the 14th century the Muzaka held the land between the rivers Devoll and Vjosë. Some served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> while others took titles such as sebastokrator from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>; Gjon I Muzaka, loyal to the Byzantines, was captured by Angevin forces in 1279 and released under pressure from Albanian nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a> became despot in 1335 but then backed an anti-Byzantine revolt and in 1336 became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Robert, Prince of Taranto. Around 1340 he defeated a Serbian army at the Pelister mountain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> took the family's domains in the 1340s, but after his death in 1355 they regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> and took Kastoria.</p><p>Andrea II's descendants divided his lands at his death in 1372, Theodor II holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myzeqe">Myzeqe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>. With Stoya Muzaka he built the Church of St Athanasius of Mouzaki in Kastoria in 1383-84, and the family chronicle has him fall at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Battle of Savra</a> in 1385 the region came under Ottoman control, and the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> and Berat ended the family's rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Some of the family converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>: Yakup Bey Muzaka was sanjakbey of the Sanjak of Albania until he was killed in 1442 by forces under Janos Hunyadi. In 1444 Theodor Corona Musachi joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s rebellion, and when the Ottomans crushed it many of the family withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while those who stayed lost their feudal rights. Theodor is remembered in South Slavic epic poetry as Korun Aramija, enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-marko">Prince Marko</a>. According to some sources the last member of the line in Italy died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1600, but in the 18th century Ahmet Pasha Kurt of the family was sanjakbey of Avlona.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muzaka family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 2 March 1444, in the Venetian-held town of Lezhë, Skanderbeg gathered the leading Albanian nobles, among them the Arianiti, Dukagjini, <b>Muzaka</b>, Thopia, Zaharia and Balšić families and Stefan Crnojević of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl ruled from Durrës and the inland town of Elbasan, rivalling Zeta under the Balšići and the <b>Muzaka of Berat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania-medieval">Principality of Albania (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Muzaka</b> were powerful Albanian nobles of the Myzeqe plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-berat">Principality of Berat</a></li></ul></section>
